<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/blog/how-to-recycle-batteries-safely/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />
  <h1>How to Recycle Batteries Safely</h1>
  <p class="lede">Batteries must never be discarded in regular household waste. Safe battery recycling starts with identifying the chemistry, storing batteries in a cool, dry place away from flammable materials, and delivering them to an authorized recycler through a scheduled pickup or drop-off.</p>

  <CtaBar whatsappMessage="Hi, I have a question about How to Recycle Batteries Safely." />

  <ul class="key-takeaways">
    <li>Common chemistries include lithium-ion (phones, laptops, EVs), lead-acid (cars, inverters), nickel-cadmium (power tools), and alkaline (household devices).</li>
    <li>Store batteries in a non-conductive container, keeping terminals from touching metal.</li>
    <li>Book a pickup with an authorized recycler for quantities above a few batteries.</li>
  </ul>

  <section>
    <h2>Identify Battery Chemistry</h2>
    <p>Common chemistries include lithium-ion (phones, laptops, EVs), lead-acid (cars, inverters), nickel-cadmium (power tools), and alkaline (household devices). Each chemistry requires different handling during transport and processing — lithium batteries are particularly sensitive to heat and puncture.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Safe Storage Before Recycling</h2>
    <p>Store batteries in a non-conductive container, keeping terminals from touching metal. Tape lithium battery terminals to prevent short circuits. Keep batteries in a cool, dry place away from direct sunlight and flammable materials.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Transport and Pickup</h2>
    <p>Book a pickup with an authorized recycler for quantities above a few batteries. For small quantities, drop off at a designated CPCB-authorised collection point. Never transport damaged or swollen batteries in your vehicle's passenger compartment.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details>
  <summary>Can I put batteries in regular trash?</summary>
  <p>No. Batteries are classified as hazardous waste under E-Waste Rules 2022 and must be recycled through authorized channels.</p>
</details>
  <details>
  <summary>What if my battery is swollen?</summary>
  <p>Swollen or leaking batteries should be flagged separately when booking. Transport them in a fireproof container and avoid puncturing.</p>
</details>
  <details>
  <summary>Are car batteries accepted?</summary>
  <p>Yes, lead-acid car batteries are accepted at authorized recyclers. They are among the most efficiently recycled battery types.</p>
</details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Related Resources</h2>
    <ul class="related-links">
      <li><a href="/battery-recycling/">Battery Recycling Services</a></li>
      <li><a href="/blog/lithium-ion-battery-disposal/">Lithium Ion Battery Disposal</a></li>
      <li><a href="/blog/what-is-lithium-battery-recycling/">What Is Lithium Battery Recycling?</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <RelatedContent href="/blog/how-to-recycle-batteries-safely/" />
</Layout>